An EEG decoder for a lower-limb exoskeleton is fitted once but must work
for weeks, while changes in electrode impedance and montage shift
the covariance its spatial filters depend on. Re-estimating the input covariance
at inference and whitening by it, without labels, corrects this shift inside the
network. Under a protocol that presents classes in long blocks, however, the
running estimate converges to the active class and whitens away the signal it
should protect.

We present a 24{,}181-parameter decoder built around such a layer, and derive a
lower bound on its adaptation rate from the class-block timescale of the
recording protocol. The layer reduces session-to-session covariance distance by
$45.9 \pm 4.9\,\%$ and $62.5 \pm 12.2\,\%$ on two cohorts, in 15 of 15
participants. On a seven-participant exoskeleton cohort the decoder reaches
$0.868 \pm 0.026$ balanced accuracy over three seeds, level with eight published
decoders, and no component, alignment included, is shown to change accuracy
relative to the convolutional stem alone. On a treadmill cohort whose class
blocks last 309 windows, the decoder transfers at $0.729$ without the layer over three seeds, and enabling the layer costs $0.149$,
lower in 8 of 8 participants. The lower bound then predicted a third cohort in
advance: both registered predictions held, and the decoder reached $0.796$
against $0.783$ for the strongest published decoder, with calibration error
$0.061$. The upper bound, set by the drift timescale, was not measurable; the lower
bound can be checked from the recording protocol before training.
